A projected annual budget surplus does not guarantee that cash will be available when specific bills come due, because farm income and expenses rarely align on a monthly basis. To determine if money will arrive in time for spring bills and the autumn loan payment, you must move beyond the annual profit figure and create a monthly cash flow projection. This involves listing every expected cash inflow (such as crop sales, livestock sales, or government payments) and every cash outflow (including seed, fertilizer, fuel, insurance, and loan principal/interest) for each month of the year, then calculating the running cash balance to identify any months where the balance would drop below zero.

The critical step is to match the timing of your income against the timing of your obligations, as a surplus can still result in a cash shortfall if large expenses occur before major revenue is received. For a Kansas operation, this often means checking if spring input costs are covered by existing cash reserves or if you need to pre-sell grain or secure a short-term operating line of credit before those bills hit. Similarly, you must verify if the autumn loan payment can be met with harvest revenue or if you need to adjust the timing of grain sales to ensure funds are liquid by the due date.

To finalize this plan, you need to gather your specific records for expected sale dates, contract prices, and exact payment due dates, as generic regional data cannot predict your specific cash timing. Without these specific dates and amounts, it is not possible to confirm whether the surplus will cover the timing gap. Once you have these details, run the monthly projection;

